    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>In the intellectual and cultural ferment of fifteenth-century Florence, the emergence of linear perspective marked a profound epistemological transformation that extended far beyond visual technique. Following the pioneering optical demonstrations of architect Filippo Brunelleschi at the Baptistery of San Giovanni, Leon Battista Alberti codified the mathematical principles of artificial perspective in his monumental 1435 treatise, <i>De pictura</i> (On Painting). Alberti conceptualized the painted surface as a <i>finestra aperta</i>&mdash;an open window through which the viewer gazes upon an illusory, three-dimensional spatial continuum constructed according to rigorous Euclidean geometry. This optical revolution fundamentally shattered the long-dominant medieval pictorial tradition. In medieval Byzantine and Gothic art, spatial organization was governed by hieratic scaling: figures were proportioned not according to optical distance, but according to their spiritual rank and theological majesty. In these devotional panels, Christ or the Virgin Mary towered majestically over mortal saints, martyrs, and humble donors in a flattened, timeless symbolic realm deliberately devoid of measurable earthly depth or empirical geography.</p>\n<p>The mathematical apparatus of Alberti&rsquo;s <i>costruzione legittima</i> achieved this spatial illusion through a coordinated geometric grid. Orthogonal lines, representing parallel lines extending perpendicularly into depth, converge inexorably at a single vanishing point (<i>punto centrico</i>) situated directly along the horizon line at the observer&rsquo;s eye level, while transversal lines diminish proportionally as they recede into the visual distance. However, linear perspective was never a purely mechanical exercise in architectural drafting; it was the quintessential visual manifestation of Renaissance Humanism. By anchoring the entire geometry of depicted space to a solitary, stationary vantage point, Alberti&rsquo;s system established an immutable reciprocity between the external world and the individual observer. The universe was visually restructured to emanate from, and converge upon, the sovereign human eye. In place of the medieval theocentric cosmos, linear perspective enthroned human consciousness as the central organizing axis of observable reality, perfectly reflecting the humanist conviction that man is the measure of all things.</p>\n<p>This triumphant narrative of objective scientific progress was famously problematized in the twentieth century by art historian Erwin Panofsky in his landmark 1927 study, <i>Perspective as Symbolic Form</i>. Panofsky challenged the widespread assumption that linear perspective constitutes an innocent, unmediated reproduction of natural human vision. In physiological reality, human eyesight is binocular, dynamic, and curvilinear; optical images are cast across a curved retina, producing peripheral distortion that our mobile eyes continuously correct through saccadic movements. Alberti&rsquo;s perspective, by contrast, requires a monocular, stationary, and planar abstraction that freezes reality into a static cone of vision. Panofsky argued that linear perspective is not an absolute physiological truth, but a &ldquo;symbolic form&rdquo;&mdash;a culturally contingent aesthetic convention through which a specific historical era translates its philosophical worldview into visual structure. By translating lived, psycho-physiological space into an infinite, homogeneous, and isotropic mathematical coordinate system, the Renaissance codified an ideology of rationalized mastery over nature.</p>\n<p>Subsequent critical theorists have extended Panofsky&rsquo;s critique, interrogating what cultural historian Martin Jay terms &ldquo;Cartesian perspectivalism.&rdquo; These scholars argue that the perspectival regime fostered a detached, disembodied gaze that treats the visible world as an inert, quantifiable spectacle arrayed for the sovereign spectator&rsquo;s intellectual domination. This visual paradigm has been persuasively linked to modern surveillance, scientific detachment, and colonial cartography, wherein the viewing subject assumes an unreciprocated, God-like authority over the observed object. Despite these ideological critiques, Alberti&rsquo;s conceptual breakthrough remains decisive: by fusing empirical observation with mathematical order, linear perspective inaugurated the visual language of modernity, forever redefining how Western culture envisions the cosmos and humanity&rsquo;s place within it.</p>",
