    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>In the wake of the Protestant Reformation, the Roman Catholic Church confronted an existential crisis that threatened its spiritual hegemony across Europe. Protestant reformers had launched fierce theological attacks against the veneration of images, condemning religious iconography as idolatrous superstition and championing the austere primacy of the unadorned Word. Convening in its twenty-fifth and final session in 1563, the Council of Trent issued definitive decrees regulating sacred art. The Church declared that religious paintings and sculptures were indispensable instruments of spiritual pedagogy and pastoral propaganda. However, the Tridentine decrees firmly proscribed the intellectualized complexities and pagan mythological allegories that had characterized late Renaissance Mannerism. Instead, Counter-Reformation art was mandated to be lucid, doctrinally orthodox, and emotionally visceral, engineered to evoke immediate piety, awe, and penitent tears among the illiterate faithful. Out of this theological imperative emerged the dramatic visual rhetoric of the Baroque.</p>\n<p>No artist embodied or revolutionized this mandate more radically than Michelangelo Merisi da Caravaggio. Arriving in Rome at the twilight of the sixteenth century, Caravaggio transformed European painting through his extreme deployment of chiaroscuro, a technique known as <i>tenebrism</i> (derived from the Italian <i>tenebroso</i>, meaning dark or gloomy). Whereas High Renaissance masters such as Raphael had utilized balanced, diffuse lighting to harmonize figures within serene rational geometry, Caravaggio plunged his pictorial backgrounds into impenetrable, pitch-black voids. Into this suffocating darkness, he slashed dramatic, directional beams of harsh theatrical illumination that isolated key figures and heightened dramatic tension. More disruptively, Caravaggio abandoned the classical tradition of idealized beauty in favor of an unvarnished, aggressive naturalism. Rather than painting ethereal saints crowned with golden haloes, Caravaggio recruited impoverished day laborers, street urchins, and courtesans from the Roman underbelly to serve as his biblical models, depicting them with dirt-caked fingernails, bare soiled feet, and leathery, weathered brows.</p>\n<p>Caravaggio&rsquo;s explosive aesthetic generated intense ambivalence among his ecclesiastical patrons. On one hand, his stark tenebrism perfectly satisfied the Counter-Reformation demand for sensational immediacy. In masterpieces such as <i>The Calling of Saint Matthew</i> and <i>The Incredulity of Saint Thomas</i>, divine illumination penetrates the ordinary world with breathtaking somatic shock, compelling the viewer to witness holy mysteries not as distant historical allegories, but as urgent, tangible events occurring in real time. On the other hand, his refusal to idealize sacred bodies repeatedly scandalized ecclesiastical authorities. Several of his most ambitious public commissions were unceremoniously rejected by church friars who deemed his plebeian realism sacrilegious. The most notorious scandal surrounded <i>The Death of the Virgin</i>, rejected by the Carmelite monks of Santa Maria della Scala because Caravaggio depicted the Mother of God not ascending gracefully into heaven, but lying swollen and barefoot as a drowned corpse, reportedly modeled upon a known prostitute retrieved from the Tiber River. To orthodox clerical eyes, Caravaggio had transgressed the sacrosanct boundary between divine transcendence and vulgar profanity.</p>\n<p>Modern art historians emphasize that Caravaggio&rsquo;s revolutionary chiaroscuro was far more than theatrical showmanship or crude institutional propaganda. As scholar Walter Friedlaender has observed, Caravaggio&rsquo;s tenebrism functions as a profound theological and existential metaphor. Divine grace does not illuminate an already harmonious, virtuous world; rather, it erupts violently into the suffocating darkness of mortal sin, bodily vulnerability, and existential abandonment. By grounding the divine directly within the physical squalor of human mortality, Caravaggio captured the profound anxieties of the early modern era, demonstrating that spiritual redemption is most intensely experienced at the precise threshold where earthly flesh confronts oblivion.</p>",
