    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Throughout the eighteenth century, English literary culture was dominated by the Neoclassical, or Augustan, aesthetic orthodoxy epitomized by Alexander Pope, John Dryden, and Samuel Johnson. In the Neoclassical worldview, poetry was conceived as an aristocratic, highly disciplined craft rooted in classical antiquity, intellectual wit, and formal symmetry. Poetic excellence depended upon adherence to strict prescriptive rules, exemplified by the relentless precision of the rhymed heroic couplet. Furthermore, the Augustan tradition mandated an elevated &ldquo;poetic diction&rdquo;&mdash;a specialized vocabulary characterized by Latinate inversions, mythological personifications, and ornate circumlocutions designed to distinguish verse from ordinary speech. Under Pope&rsquo;s famous dictum, &ldquo;True Wit is Nature to advantage dress&rsquo;d, / What oft was thought, but ne&rsquo;er so well express&rsquo;d,&rdquo; the poet functioned as a sophisticated civic craftsman whose primary duty was to articulate universal moral truths, celebrate social order, and refine public taste through rational polish.</p>\n<p>This rationalist consensus was radically challenged at the century&rsquo;s close by the publication of William Wordsworth and Samuel Taylor Coleridge&rsquo;s <i>Lyrical Ballads</i> in 1798, accompanied by Wordsworth&rsquo;s revolutionary Preface to the 1800 and 1802 editions. Wordsworth staged an uncompromising revolt against what he deemed the sterile, mechanical artificiality of Neoclassical verse. Rejecting the notion that poetry required specialized aristocratic ornament, Wordsworth declared that the poet should choose incidents and situations from &ldquo;humble and rustic life,&rdquo; where the essential passions of the human heart are less restrained and speak a more direct, durable language. Crucially, he demanded that poetry adopt a selection of the &ldquo;language really used by men&rdquo;&mdash;a living vernacular uncorrupted by arbitrary poetic idioms. For Wordsworth, poetry was no longer a decorative exercise in intellectual craftsmanship, but the &ldquo;spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Yet, while popular caricatures often equate Romanticism with uncontrolled sentimental indulgence, Wordsworth&rsquo;s aesthetic doctrine rested upon a sophisticated psychological paradox. He immediately qualified his definition of spontaneity by asserting that poetry &ldquo;takes its origin from emotion recollected in tranquility.&rdquo; In Wordsworth&rsquo;s cognitive model, the raw, instantaneous shock of experience cannot be directly transcribed into art at the moment of affective excitation. Instead, the initial emotional surge must be revisited through retrospective contemplation in peaceful solitude. The poet ponders the past experience until, through sustained imaginative recollection, a kindred secondary emotion is generated within the mind, enabling disciplined formal composition. Romantic spontaneity is thus balanced by rigorous psychological and aesthetic mediation; passion is not eradicated, but distilled into enduring contemplation.</p>\n<p>Wordsworth&rsquo;s manifesto provoked sharp critical dissent, notably from his own collaborator Coleridge. In <i>Biographia Literaria</i> (1817), Coleridge forcefully disputed the claim that rustic speech possessed an innate poetic superiority. Coleridge observed that uneducated laborers possessed limited vocabularies shaped by narrow survival needs, and that Wordsworth&rsquo;s finest poems achieved their lyrical majesty not by transcribing rustic dialect, but by employing the philosophical vocabulary of an educated, contemplative mind. Modern historicist critics, such as Jerome McGann in <i>The Romantic Ideology</i>, have mounted an even sharper political critique. They argue that Wordsworth&rsquo;s pastoral idealization of rustic simplicity operated as an ideological evasion. By recasting rural figures into timeless symbols of spiritual endurance, Wordsworth effectively obscured the brutal material realities of rural poverty, agrarian enclosure, and capitalist displacement during the Industrial Revolution. Nonetheless, Wordsworth&rsquo;s fundamental triumph remains undeniable: by asserting the primacy of the subjective interior, he dismantled Neoclassical aristocratic decorum and forever expanded the democratic horizons of literary expression.</p>",
