    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In the intellectual and artistic landscape of mid-nineteenth-century Victorian Britain, George Eliot (the pen name of Mary Ann Evans) formulated the definitive moral and philosophical manifesto of psychological realism. In her celebrated 1856 essay, &ldquo;The Natural History of German Life,&rdquo; Eliot declared that &ldquo;the greatest benefit we owe to the artist, whether painter, poet, or novelist, is the extension of our sympathies.&rdquo; For Eliot, the realist novel was never an escapist romantic fantasy, nor was it a vehicle for heavy-handed moralistic preaching or didactic melodrama. Rather, it operated as a sacred instrument of ethical awakening and civic education. Realism demanded the uncompromisingly truthful, affectionate representation of ordinary, unheroic, and deeply flawed human beings. By illuminating the quiet, unglamorous struggles of provincial villagers, artisans, impoverished curates, and disillusioned scholars, the realist novelist shatters habitual egoism, compelling readers to cultivate expansive empathy for fellow human beings whose temperaments, social classes, and moral shortcomings diverge radically from their own.</p>\n<p>Eliot&rsquo;s aesthetic philosophy achieved its monumental realization in her 1871 masterpiece, <i>Middlemarch: A Study of Provincial Life</i>. At the center of the novel&rsquo;s sociological vision is the famous metaphor of the &ldquo;web.&rdquo; Eliot insists that no human life can be understood in heroic isolation; rather, every individual destiny is inexorably woven into a dense, vibrating matrix of provincial traditions, family obligations, economic dependencies, political rivalries, and collective gossip. This web of social determination is vividly enacted through the tragedy of Dorothea Brooke. An ardent, idealistic young woman harboring noble spiritual aspirations reminiscent of Saint Theresa, Dorothea finds her expansive vision inexorably suffocated by her pedantic, emotionally desiccated husband, Edward Casaubon, and the petty conformism of provincial society. Similarly, the brilliant young physician Tertius Lydgate discovers his reformist scientific ambitions gradually corrupted by financial vanity, social snobbery, and his shallow marriage to Rosamond Vincy. Eliot systematically demonstrates how high moral ideals are inevitably shaped, compromised, and circumscribed by mundane material reality.</p>\n<p>To orchestrate this intricate panoramic canvas, Eliot employs an authoritative, omniscient narrative voice. Yet Eliot&rsquo;s omniscience is fundamentally distinct from cold, panoptic surveillance; it is saturated with philosophical wisdom, gentle irony, and profound compassion. Utilizing free indirect discourse (<i>style indirect libre</i>)&mdash;wherein the narrator seamlessly slips between objective third-person narration and the subjective interior consciousness of a character&mdash;Eliot reveals the competing rationalizations and private griefs animating each actor. The narrator refuses to reduce characters to facile caricatures of virtue or villainy. Even the bitter, insecure Casaubon or the narcissistic, manipulative Rosamond are afforded moments of microscopic interior illumination, exposing their desperate human vulnerability and forbidding the reader from passing facile, self-righteous condemnation upon their frailties.</p>\n<p>Despite Eliot&rsquo;s towering authority, twentieth-century modernists and poststructuralist theorists subjected realist omniscience to rigorous critique. Modernists such as Virginia Woolf contended that the Victorian all-knowing narrator was an artificial, paternalistic construct that imposed a false, mechanical coherence upon the chaotic, fluid fragmentation of human inner life. Poststructuralists like Roland Barthes and D.A. Miller argued that the omniscient perspective acted as a panoptic ideological apparatus of bourgeois social discipline, subtly reinforcing normative class and gender hierarchies under the guise of neutral, God-like objectivity. In response, contemporary defenders maintain that Eliot&rsquo;s narrative stance was never authoritarian. Rather, by tirelessly staging the partiality and blindness of every individual perspective within the social web, Eliot modeled a democratic, epistemological humility, providing an indispensable ethical antidote to dogmatism and ideological fanaticism.</p>",
