    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 8 (Questions 43&ndash;48)</div>\n<p>In her radical 1919 manifesto &ldquo;Modern Fiction,&rdquo; Virginia Woolf launched a devastating critique against the dominant Edwardian realist novelists&mdash;Arnold Bennett, John Galsworthy, and H.G. Wells. Woolf labeled these celebrated authors &ldquo;materialists,&rdquo; accusing them of expending immense craftsmanship constructing solid, external brick-and-mortar facades while letting the vital essence of life slip through their fingers. The Edwardians meticulously inventoried household furniture, legal property, financial ledgers, and social etiquette, laboring under the stubborn Victorian illusion that compiling outward physical details constituted authentic realism. For Woolf, however, reality resided elsewhere: &ldquo;Life is not a series of gig lamps symmetrically arranged; life is a luminous halo, a semi-transparent envelope surrounding us from the beginning of consciousness to the end.&rdquo; To capture this shimmering, fleeting interiority, the novelist had to liberate fiction from the tyranny of external chronological plot, recording instead the &ldquo;incessant shower of innumerable atoms&rdquo; as they imprint themselves upon the human mind in all their chaotic unpredictability.</p>\n<p>This aesthetic revolution found deep philosophical resonance in Henri Bergson&rsquo;s critique of scientific temporality. Bergson posited a radical dichotomy between spatialized mechanical time and subjective <i>dur&eacute;e</i> (duration). Mechanical clock time is quantitative, homogeneous, and divisible&mdash;a spatialized construct that parcels human experience into uniform, interchangeable seconds, minutes, and hours. In contrast, <i>dur&eacute;e</i> is qualitative, continuous, and indivisible, representing the organic, flowing tapestry of inner consciousness where past memories, present sensations, and future anticipations interpenetrate without artificial borders. In the stream-of-consciousness technique, as perfected in Woolf&rsquo;s <i>Mrs. Dalloway</i> (1925) and <i>To the Lighthouse</i> (1927), narrative time completely decouples from linear chronology. Through free indirect interior monologue and involuntary associative memory, an instantaneous sensory cue&mdash;the fragrance of morning air, the striking of a match, or the dipping of an oar&mdash;can expand into pages of introspective excavation, collapsing decades into an expansive, luminous &ldquo;moment of being.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Yet Woolf does not dissolve narrative entirely into solipsistic reverie; rather, her fiction stages a relentless, tragic confrontation between internal subjective duration and external institutional temporality. Throughout <i>Mrs. Dalloway</i>, the relentless striking of Big Ben resounds across the streets of London, its &ldquo;leaden circles&rdquo; dissolving in the air. Big Ben functions as the acoustic emblem of the British imperial state, embodying the rigid, patriarchal authority that ruthlessly regiments civic life, imperial governance, commercial markets, and medical orthodoxy. This mechanical time collides catastrophically with the shattered, traumatized psyche of Septimus Warren Smith, a shell-shocked veteran of the Great War. Septimus&rsquo;s agonizing trench flashbacks refuse to submit to tidy civilian chronology. When authoritarian medical psychiatrists attempt to coerce him into institutional conformity and emotional submission, Septimus leaps from a window to his death&mdash;a desperate, heroic act of defiance asserting the inviolable sanctity of private consciousness against imperial temporal regimentation.</p>\n<p>Woolf&rsquo;s formal experimentation has provoked sharply conflicting ideological evaluations among twentieth-century theorists. The Marxist philosopher Georg Luk&aacute;cs notoriously condemned modernist stream of consciousness as a decadent retreat into subjective pathology. Luk&aacute;cs argued that by atomizing objective social reality into private psychological fragments, modernists abdicated literature&rsquo;s historical duty to expose the socioeconomic contradictions of capitalism. Conversely, contemporary feminist and anti-imperialist critics interpret Woolf&rsquo;s temporal distortions as deeply subversive political interventions. By dismantling linear, teleological narrative progression, Woolf disrupted the patriarchal myth of inevitable national progress that had culminated in the industrial slaughter of the First World War. Rather than an escapist retreat, Woolf&rsquo;s fluid temporal aesthetic creates an empathetic intersubjective refuge that preserves human intimacy against bureaucratic totalization.</p>",
