    "id": "exam_cars90_4_p9",
    "sec": "CARS",
    "topic": "Postmodern Metafiction & Narrative Self-Reflexivity / Italo Calvino",
    "sheet": null,
    "label": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills",
    "examId": "cars90_4",
    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 9 (Questions 49&ndash;53)</div>\n<p>In the latter half of the twentieth century, the landscape of Western literature witnessed a radical departure from traditional storytelling through the rise of postmodern metafiction. Pioneered by visionary writers such as Jorge Luis Borges, John Barth, and Italo Calvino, metafiction is broadly defined as fiction that self-consciously and systematically draws attention to its own status as an artistic artifact. Whereas nineteenth-century realism labored to construct a seamless, transparent window through which readers could observe an ostensibly unmediated earthly reality, metafiction violently shatters this mimetic illusion. Metafictive texts deliberately expose the internal scaffolding of storytelling, reminding the reader that the narrative universe is not a natural slice of life, but a highly contrived linguistic construction governed by arbitrary literary conventions, generic codes, and rhetorical maneuvers.</p>\n<p>The supreme exemplar of narrative self-reflexivity is Italo Calvino&rsquo;s 1979 masterpiece, <i>If on a winter&rsquo;s night a traveler</i>. Calvino audaciously opens the novel with a direct second-person address that implicates the physical reader in the immediate act of consumption: &ldquo;You are about to begin reading Italo Calvino&rsquo;s new novel, <i>If on a winter&rsquo;s night a traveler</i>.&rdquo; The protagonist of the framing story is none other than &ldquo;the Reader,&rdquo; who purchases the book only to discover that due to a printer&rsquo;s binder error, the text abruptly cuts off after the opening chapter. The Reader&rsquo;s quest to obtain a complete copy plunges him into an elusive labyrinth containing ten distinct, uncompleted novel beginnings, each parodying a different literary genre&mdash;ranging from neo-noir espionage and European existential malaise to magic realism and Latin American revolutionary political melodrama. By making the desire for narrative closure the central engine of the plot, Calvino dismantles the classical hierarchy between omnipotent author and passive consumer. The reader is transformed from an obedient recipient of authorial wisdom into an active, self-aware collaborator in the collaborative production of literary meaning.</p>\n<p>This narrative self-reflexivity is deeply aligned with poststructuralist critical theory, particularly Roland Barthes&rsquo;s celebrated proclamation of the &ldquo;Death of the Author.&rdquo; Poststructuralism vigorously rejects the humanist assumption that the text is the sovereign property of an individual creative genius who implants an authoritative, singular meaning. When Calvino lays bare how suspense, romantic intrigue, and narrative tension are mechanically generated through generic formulas, he affirms Barthes&rsquo;s thesis that a text is not a closed theological statement, but a multidimensional space wherein diverse linguistic traditions, citations, and cultural codes blend and clash. Narrative authority is radicalized and dispersed; meaning does not reside immutably on the printed page, but is continuously co-created and perpetually deferred in the dynamic interpretive act of reading.</p>\n<p>Inevitably, postmodern metafiction has elicited sharp resistance from literary moralists and traditional critics. Theorists such as John Gardner and Fredric Jameson argued that metafiction threatened to reduce literature to sterile, narcissistic intellectual play. By relentlessly ironizing its own devices, critics warned, metafiction severed art from ethical engagement, emotional vulnerability, and urgent historical reality, abandoning the real world in favor of an arid hall of linguistic mirrors. Yet defenders of Calvino maintain that his self-reflexivity was never cynical navel-gazing. Rather, Calvino&rsquo;s playful exposure of literary machinery is animated by boundless affection, erotic wonder, and a profound reverence for storytelling itself. By honestly confessing its own artifice, metafiction liberates narrative from ideological deception, renewing the reader&rsquo;s sense of enchantment and celebrating the infinite generativity of the human imagination.</p>",
